{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1552527-mummy-the-ressurrection-assorted-questions","id":1552527,"title":"[Mummy the Ressurrection] Assorted questions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-07-19T05:52:13-07:00","id":1552527,"title":"[Mummy the Ressurrection] Assorted questions","content":"Hello, everyone.<br /> <br /> I was reading the <i>Mummy: the Resurrection</i> core book (terrific setting, by the way. I loved the hopeful tone of the book). I've just read chapters 4 and 5, and now I have some questions regarding character creation, so maybe you might help me to understand the rules better.<br /> <br /> <b>1.</b> According to the Amenti Character Creation table, new Mummy PCs start with Balance 1 and Sekhem 1. The same table also says that &quot;Characters cannot start with a Balance rating higher than three dots&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, my question is: I can raise both traits with freebie points during character creation, but a starting Mummy cannot have Balance or Sekhem higher than three, right?<br /> <br /> <b>2.</b> Later, the book states that &quot;Your mummy\u2019s starting Sekhem score equals his final Balance rating&quot;. Since initial Balance cannot be higher than three, I cannot spend freebies to raise my initial Sekhem trait above my initial Balance trait?<br /> <br /> <b>3.</b> The Experience Costs table on page 147 does not list an experience point cost to raise the Sekhem trait. Is it possible to raise Sekhem with experience? If yes, how much does it cost? Does it go up automatically whenever I raise my Balance? Or is it raised through the game, just like Backgrounds?<br /> <br /> <b>4.</b> Last question (for now): Balance is a Mummy's moral compass and the equivalent of a Vampire's Humanity (or Road). Just like Humanity, it also has a list of offenses / sins.<br /> <br /> However, as far as I remember, in vampire whenever a PC commits a sin he must make a Conscience Roll and if he fails he loses a point of Humanity.<br /> <br /> However, since mummies have no Virtues, how exactly does this works, rules wise, for the Amenti's offenses against Ma'at? Does the storyteller just remove a point of Balance after warning the player that her intended action might constitute an offense? Or is there a system described elsewhere in the book?<br /> <br /> Thanks in advance for your answers.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28735,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","user":"Greywolf76","date":". 07-19-2025, 01:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-19T23:43:20-07:00","id":1552534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Greywolf76</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1552527-mummy-the-ressurrection-assorted-questions#post1552527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello, everyone.<br /> <br /> I was reading the <i>Mummy: the Resurrection</i> core book (terrific setting, by the way. I loved the hopeful tone of the book). I've just read chapters 4 and 5, and now I have some questions regarding character creation, so maybe you might help me to understand the rules better.<br /> <br /> <b>1.</b> According to the Amenti Character Creation table, new Mummy PCs start with Balance 1 and Sekhem 1. The same table also says that &quot;Characters cannot start with a Balance rating higher than three dots&quot;.<br /> <br /> So, my question is: I can raise both traits with freebie points during character creation, but a starting Mummy cannot have Balance or Sekhem higher than three, right?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Yes<br /> Even if you spend Freebie Points, those can't be raised above 3 during character creation.<br /> For beginning Amenti characters that is a hard cap so to speak\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Greywolf76</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1552527-mummy-the-ressurrection-assorted-questions#post1552527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello, everyone.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>2.</b> Later, the book states that &quot;Your mummy\u2019s starting Sekhem score equals his final Balance rating&quot;. Since initial Balance cannot be higher than three, I cannot spend freebies to raise my initial Sekhem trait above my initial Balance trait?</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> You see it right!<br /> You cannot raise Sekhem separately at character creation \u2014 it always equals Balance and since Balance can't go above 3 at creation, Sekhem also can't exceed 3 but If you raise Balance to 2 or 3 with Freebie Points, Sekhem will automatically increase to match.<br /> Sekhem is directly tied to Balance at character creation\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Greywolf76</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1552527-mummy-the-ressurrection-assorted-questions#post1552527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello, everyone.<br /> <br /> <b>3.</b> The Experience Costs table on page 147 does not list an experience point cost to raise the Sekhem trait. Is it possible to raise Sekhem with experience? If yes, how much does it cost? Does it go up automatically whenever I raise my Balance? Or is it raised through the game, just like Backgrounds?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>You are on a roll here! Right again!<br /> Sekhem is not raised with XP like normal Traits, instead, increases only when Balance increases, and cannot be directly bought with XP<br /> It can increase automatically with Balance... or as a result of in-game events<br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Greywolf76</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1552527-mummy-the-ressurrection-assorted-questions#post1552527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello, everyone.<br /> <br /> <b>4.</b> Last question (for now): Balance is a Mummy's moral compass and the equivalent of a Vampire's Humanity (or Road). Just like Humanity, it also has a list of offenses / sins.<br /> <br /> However, as far as I remember, in vampire whenever a PC commits a sin he must make a Conscience Roll and if he fails he loses a point of Humanity.<br /> <br /> However, since mummies have no Virtues, how exactly does this works, rules wise, for the Amenti's offenses against Ma'at? Does the storyteller just remove a point of Balance after warning the player that her intended action might constitute an offense? Or is there a system described elsewhere in the book?<br /> <br /> Thanks in advance for your answers.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the hardest one<br /> Balance is the measure of how well one of the Amenti understands the nature of Ma'at. It is somewhat analogous to a vampire's Humanity, but not quite like Humanity. Balance measures the strength of his understanding and relationship with universal justice, balance and rightness - the understanding of Ma'at (not the Goddess, the concept, tje oppostie the Isfet. Ma'at represents order, truth, justice, and balance, while Isfet embodies chaos, injustice, violence, and evil)<br /> The udnerstanding of this universal principle is the source of their power, the real soruce of their power (you can have Attribute ratings equal to his Balance score, so a character whose Balance goes above five can spend experience to increase his Attributes to superhuman levels. Similarly, a mummy's magical skill it limited by his adherence to the path of Ma'at. No Hekau can exceed his Balance score) and that is why it is limited at character creation. As you study understand more, as you prove yourself worthy in the eyes of the Judges you can strengthen and deepen his understanding , your Balance rises and thus your Sekhem rises too (there are great roleplay opportunities in how Sekhem awakening parallels moral ascension, just sayin')<br /> But when a mummy commits an offense against Ma\u2019at (as listed in the book by Balance rating), the Storyteller makes a judgment call. There\u2019s no dice roll involved - unless the Storyteller includes some kind of house made custom mechanic - and if the offense is serious enough \u2014 and deliberate \u2014 the Storyteller can rule that the character loses a point of Balance representing when a mummy strays from balance, they may be judged by the Judges of Ma'at, who will assess their actions and potentially impose penalties (of course, one may attempt to escape the judgment of Ma'at by severing ties with the Judges... good luck with that and with the consequances)<br /> Balance is, in essence, Spiritual Evolution<br /> You must prove yourself to the gods and the cosmic order and raising it's - or losing it - is not just a stat but a measure of a character\u2019s spiritual alignment and udnerstanding with one of the universe' prime principle, the Ma\u2019at.<br /> \u200b\u200bLosing it and rising it is part of the story, part of your life and path as an Immortal - Osiris, Ma'at, the Gods and the Judges does not really tolerate the abuse of eternal life you got from them, your &quot;second chance&quot;; after all your a flawed human has been made &quot;whole&quot; by the fragment of an ancient egyptian Soul (the Egyptians believed a person was composed of nine different parts:<br /> Khat, the Physical Body - this one doesn not, for obvious reason -have a soul fragment, Ab, the Physical Heart, the seat of consciousness - again, no fragment, Khu, the physical and mystical representation of a being, akin to an Aura, that ties the spiritual and material parts together; this part makes a Sakhmu, Ba, the part of the soul that ventures into the Underworld, the &quot;personality&quot;; akin to a Wraith; this part makes a Khri-Habi, Khaibit, the Shadow, which represent the darker urges and emotions of the soul, akin to the Shadow of the Wraiths, P'o of the Kuei Jin and may or may not the Beast of the Vampires; it makes a Sefekhi, Ka, the part of the soul that remains close to the body and guards it from desecration &quot;vital essence&quot;; this part makes Kher-minu, Sahu, the indestructible part of the soul that will dwell in the fields of A'aru, akin to the Eidolon, pure intellect; it makes a Mesektet and the Ren, the True Name, a vital part of existence and the soul, a &quot;brief&quot; summary of what the person is. Between these parts flows Sekhem, the force of life itself that binds the other soul-parts together. )<br /> <br /> The Judges are part of the cosmic order that enforces of the Ma\u2019at and they are watching you like a hawk through the eyes of your Storyteller, they are cosmic enforcers and arbiters, not just after death, but throughout your mummy\u2019s immortal existence. The 42 Judges are divine beings who represent specific moral principles or sins. Each one questions the soul of the deceased, asking if they committed a specific offense. The Assessors of Maat are not explicitly named with a consistent list across all texts, but there is a list of them the Game based on:<ul><li><b>Usekh\u2011nemmt</b> (&quot;Far\u2011Strider&quot;) \u2013 <i>falsehood</i>: \u201cI have not committed sin.\u201d</li> <li><b>Hept\u2011khet</b> (&quot;Fire\u2011Embracer&quot;) \u2013 <i>robbery with violence</i>: \u201cI have not committed robbery with violence.\u201d</li> <li><b>Fenti</b> (&quot;Nosey One&quot;) \u2013 <i>rapaciousness/theft</i>: \u201cI have not stolen.\u201d</li> <li><b>Am\u2011khaibit</b> (&quot;Swallower of Shades&quot;) \u2013 <i>murder</i>: \u201cI have not slain men and women.\u201d</li> <li><b>Neha\u2011her</b> (&quot;Dangerous One&quot;) \u2013 <i>stealing (grain)</i>: \u201cI have not stolen grain.\u201d</li> <li><b>Ruruti</b> (&quot;Double Lion&quot;) \u2013 <i>destruction of food/pilfering offerings</i>: \u201cI have not purloined offerings.\u201d</li> <li><b>Arfi\u2011em\u2011khet</b> (&quot;Fiery Eyes&quot;) \u2013 <i>stealing temple property/crookedness</i>: \u201cI have not stolen the property of God.\u201d</li> <li><b>Neba</b> (&quot;Flame&quot;) \u2013 <i>lying or stealing offerings</i> (depending on translation)</li> <li><b>Set\u2011qesu</b> (&quot;Bone Breaker&quot;) \u2013 <i>lying</i></li> <li><b>Utu\u2011nesert</b> (&quot;Green of Flame&quot;) \u2013 <i>taking food</i></li> <li><b>Qerrti</b> (&quot;You of the Cavern&quot;) \u2013 <i>sullenness</i></li> <li><b>Kenemti</b> (&quot;White of Teeth&quot;) \u2013 <i>transgression</i></li> <li><b>Hetch\u2011abhu </b>(&quot;House of Nature&quot;) \u2013 <i>killing a sacred bull </i></li> <li><b>Ta\u2011retiu</b> (&quot;Eater of Entrails&quot;) \u2013 <i>perjury</i></li> <li><b>Unem\u2011snef</b> (&quot;Lord of Truth&quot;) \u2013 <i>stealing bread</i></li> <li><b>Unem\u2011besek</b> (&quot;Wanderer&quot;) \u2013 <i>eavesdropping</i></li> <li><b>Neb\u2011Maat</b> (&quot;Pale One&quot;) \u2013 <i>babbling</i></li> <li><b>Tenemiu</b> (&quot;Doubly Evil&quot;) \u2013 <i>disputing</i></li> <li><b>Sertiu</b> (&quot;Wememty\u2011Snake&quot;) \u2013 <i>adultery</i></li> <li><b>Tutu</b> (&quot;See Whom You Bring&quot;) \u2013 <i>misbehavior</i></li> <li><b>Uamenti</b> (&quot;Over the Old One&quot;) \u2013 <i>terrorizing</i></li> <li><b>Maa\u2011antuf</b> (&quot;Demolisher&quot;) \u2013 <i>transgressing</i></li> <li><b>Her\u2011uru</b> (&quot;Disturber&quot;) \u2013 <i>being hot\u2011tempered</i></li> <li><b>Khemiu</b> (&quot;Youth&quot;) \u2013 <i>not listening to truth</i></li> <li><b>Shet\u2011kheru</b> (&quot;Foreteller&quot;) \u2013 <i>making disturbance</i></li> <li><b>Nebheru</b> (&quot;You of the Altar&quot;, from the secret place) \u2013 <i>hoodwinking</i></li> <li><b>Hraf\u2011haf</b> (&quot;Face Behind Him&quot;) \u2013 <i>copulating with a boy</i></li> <li><b>An\u2011hetep\u2011f</b> (&quot;Hot\u2011Foot&quot;) \u2013 <i>neglect</i></li> <li><b>Sera\u2011kheru</b> (&quot;You of the Darkness&quot;) \u2013 <i>quarrelling</i>: \u201cI have not been a stirrer up of strife.\u201d</li> <li><b>Neb\u2011heru</b> (&quot;Bringer of Your Offerings&quot;) \u2013 <i>acting with undue haste</i></li> <li><b>Sekhriu</b> (&quot;Owner of Faces&quot;) \u2013 <i>impatience</i>: \u201cI have not pried into others\u2019 matters.\u201d</li> <li><b>Neb\u2011abui</b> (&quot;Accuser&quot;) \u2013 <i>damaging a god\u2019s image</i>: \u201cI have not multiplied my words in speaking.\u201d</li> <li><b>Tcheser\u2011tep</b> (&quot;Owner of Horns&quot;) \u2013 <i>talkativeness</i></li> <li><b>Nefertem</b> \u2013 <i>wrongdoing/evil seeing</i>: \u201cI have wronged none, I have done no evil.\u201d</li> <li><b>Temsep / Tem\u2011Sepu</b> \u2013 <i>witchcraft against the king</i>: \u201cI have not worked witchcraft against the king.\u201d</li> <li><b>Ari\u2011em\u2011ab\u2011f</b> (&quot;You Who Acted Willfully&quot;) \u2013 <i>stopping a neighbor\u2019s water flow</i>: \u201cI have never stopped the flow of water of a neighbor.\u201d</li> <li><b>Ahi</b> (&quot;Water-Smiter&quot;) \u2013 <i>being loud\u2011voiced</i>: \u201cI have never raised my voice.\u201d</li> <li><b>Uatch\u2011rekhit</b> (&quot;Commander of Mankind&quot;) \u2013 <i>reviling God</i>: \u201cI have not cursed God.\u201d</li> <li><b>Nehebkau</b> \u2013 <i>arrogance</i>: \u201cI have not acted with arrogance.\u201d</li> <li><b>Neheb\u2011nefert</b> (&quot;Bestower of Powers&quot;) \u2013 <i>making distinctions for oneself/thieving divine sustenance</i>: \u201cI have not stolen the bread of the gods.\u201d</li> <li><b>Hetch\u2011abhu</b> (&quot;Serpent With Raised Head&quot;) \u2013 <i>dishonest wealth</i>: \u201cI have not slain the cattle belonging to the god.\u201d</li> <li><b>Neb\u2011abui</b> (&quot;Serpent Who Brings and Gives&quot;) \u2013 <i>blasphemy/contempt toward a child or local god</i>: \u201cI have not snatched away the bread of the child, nor treated with contempt the god of my city.\u201d</li> </ul>\u200bSome of them are actual gods, like Tutu, Master of the demons of Sekhmet and the wandering demons of Bastet, who is the Son of Neith, depictied as a lion with a human head, wearing the Double Feather crown, the Solar Disk and Ram Horns. As a guardian diety, originally the protector of tombs, Tutu later guarded the sleeping from danger or bad dreams. He was stated to have given protection from demons, nightmares, giving longer life and protecting people from the darkness of Duat, the Netherworld<br /> <br /> So, in short, Balance = Spiritual Evolution, Sekhem = Power Curve Control basically and they are tied together, the developers intentionally tied it to Balance. Preventing high Sekhem at start prevents characters from being supernatural demigods and similarly, as you lose Balance, your powers wither<br /> <br /> If your mummy falls below Balance 1, one of two possible outcomes occurs.<br /> The first possibility is that the Judges of Ma'at will consider the mummy unworthy of eternal life. They cast the mummy's soul to Amemait, and the mummy is no more.<br /> Now strange to mention, the game calls him Amemait, but usally this beast is know as Ammit or Ammut, a Guardian Demon, a personified monster of Egyptian mythology, the &quot;<i>devourer of the dead</i>&quot;\u200b that devours the hearts of those who stride too far from the Path of Ma'at<br /> In a sene she represents the &quot;pure&quot; concept of destruction that weeds out the wicked and enables the just to live without interference from evil<br /> In WoD she may represent an uncorrupted version of the Wyrm and/or Oblivion<br /> The second option and the only chance of the mummy's continued existence is for the Amenti to renounce Osiris and Ma'at and agree to serve Apep<br /> <br /> <br /> Balance is treated more like a supernatural ethical state \u2014 part of the cosmic order \u2014 than a human conscience so there's no need for Virtue rolls like in Vampire (that said, the severity of the act and whether the character feels remorse can influence the ST\u2019s decision (and possibly redemption arcs or quests to restore Balance)).\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-21T09:28:50-07:00","id":1552561,"title":"","content":"Wow, thanks a lot for such a thorough and detailed answer, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23890_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23890\" data-vbnamecard=\"23890\">Shadeprowler</a>. It helped me a lot!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28735,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","user":"Greywolf76","date":". 07-21-2025, 12:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76","name":"Greywolf76","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28735_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-21T10:20:15-07:00","id":1552563,"title":"","content":"Two small corrections:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1552527-mummy-the-ressurrection-assorted-questions?p=1552534#post1552534\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The Assessors of Maat are not explicitly named with a consistent list across all texts ... Some of them are actual gods</div> </div> </div> </div>1 - The Assessors of Ma'at <b>are</b> named consistently. What isn't consistent is their sins and declarations of innocence;<br /> <br /> 2 - They're <b>all</b> gods, although minor ones. What happens is that some are only known in their capacity as Judges and gods of the 42 <i>nomes</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-21T11:58:35-07:00","id":1552565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1552527-mummy-the-ressurrection-assorted-questions?p=1552534#post1552534\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Assessors of Maat are not explicitly named with a consistent list across all texts, but there is a list of them the Game based on:\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>This is not to correct what Shadeprowler wrote in terms of MtR, but I want to provide some trivia on how some of these names have been used throughout the editions. I think some readers may find it interesting.<br /> <br /> In MtR, it seems they used the names of the Assessors of Maat as actual deities.<br /> <br /> However, in Mummy 2e (1997), they state there are 42 Shemsu-Heru, the mummies who follow Horus and obey the Code of Horus. While they state that this group being numbered 42 and the Assessors of Maat numbering the same are &quot;coincidence&quot;, they provided versions of these names (along with the names of other Egyptian gods and Pharoanic royalty). I feel this may have been a nudge, nudge indicating that in the minds of the writers, the Shemsu-Heru were in fact the beings whom the mythological assessors were based on (much like many of the Egyptian gods themselves were actually mortals who became vampires, mummies, mages, etc.)<br /> <br /> In turn, in Mummy 1e we are given the names of the Bane Mummies were based on the &quot; traditional manifestations of Apophis, the Egyptian devil serpent&quot; as found in E.A. Wallis Budge's Egyptian Magic. This list of &quot;traditional manifestations of Apophis&quot; included some of the names of the Assessors of Maat. However, since Budge was an early scholar of Egyptology, much of his scholarship has been superseded, and his books - while still popular - are seen as outdated with many errors by more recent experts.<br /> <br /> Therefore, you are going to see several different creatures (Bane Mummies, Shemsu-Heru mummies, the Judges of MtR) having variants of the same name (like Hau-hra of the Backward Face (Bane Mummy), Hraf-haf (Shemsu-Heru), and the Hraf-haf (&quot;Face Behind Him&quot;) of the Judges of Maat. It's just an artifact of the game's evolution and how different writers did not consider what was used before.<br /> <br /> I did not know this myself until several people contributed to a <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1504244-the-followers-of-set-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf\">discussion on the names of the Banes Mummies on this thread</a>. It begins around post #20 so skip to that if you're interested. Special thanks to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1190-elphilm\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1190_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1190\" data-vbnamecard=\"1190\">Elphilm</a> who provided a lot of detail.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28735-greywolf76\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar28735_1.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28735\" data-vbnamecard=\"28735\">Greywolf76</a> did comment on that thread (as did others on here), so he likely remembers this. I just mention it for anyone else who may not be aware.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-21T12:05:45-07:00","id":1552566,"title":"","content":"Just to clarify one thing that may confuse some:<br /> <br /> There are no inconsistencies in the list of names of the Assessors of Ma'at IRL, but there are differences in how to transliterate and/or pronounce their names, which may result in variations of the same name (the same can be applied to some of the aspects of the soul).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}